From: A50 port <noreply@example.com>
Date: Tue, 9 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fimc-is: clear device->vctx on sensor close

Suspending this device panics the SoC. Measured with pm_test: the freezer
phase completes cleanly, the devices phase kills it every time, and the
bootloader's own crash record names the site exactly:

  "PC":"fimc_is_devicemgr_open+0x1e8/0x3a0"
  "LR":"fimc_is_devicemgr_open+0x178/0x3a0"
  "FAULT":"0xffffff800c01b3b0"
  "RR":"KP"   "PANIC":"Fatal exception"

Disassembling that offset in the built vmlinux gives:

  +0x1e4:  ldr x8, [x19, #272]     ; x8 = sensor->vctx
  +0x1e8:  str x22, [x8, #9136]    ; sensor->vctx->next_device = ischain

i.e. the store immediately after fimc_is_group_open() returns. Offset 272
is confirmed as vctx by the earlier FIMC_BUG(!sensor->vctx) check at
+0x40, which loads the same offset and branches on zero.

So vctx is non-NULL - it passes that guard - and still faults when
dereferenced. It is a dangling pointer, not a null one, which is why the
fault address is a plausible-looking kernel address rather than 0.

The cause is that device->vctx is assigned in exactly one place, in
fimc_is_sensor_open(), and is never cleared anywhere. fimc_is_sensor_close()
tears down the video context but leaves device->vctx pointing at it. Any
subsequent read sees freed memory, and the vendor's own NULL guard cannot
catch it because the pointer is not NULL.

Clearing it on close restores that guard: a stale open then returns
-EINVAL through FIMC_BUG() instead of writing into freed memory. This is
the vendor's existing defensive intent, not a new mechanism - USE_FIMC_BUG
is defined for this tree, so FIMC_BUG() returns -EINVAL rather than
BUG_ON()ing.
--- a/drivers/media/platform/exynos/fimc-is2/fimc-is-device-sensor_v2.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/exynos/fimc-is2/fimc-is-device-sensor_v2.c
@@ -1913,7 +1913,14 @@
 	clear_bit(FIMC_IS_SENSOR_ITF_REGISTER, &device->state);
 	set_bit(FIMC_IS_SENSOR_BACK_NOWAIT_STOP, &device->state);
 
 p_err:
+	/*
+	 * Set only in fimc_is_sensor_open() and never cleared, so it outlived
+	 * the video context it points at. FIMC_BUG(!sensor->vctx) in
+	 * fimc_is_devicemgr_open() is a NULL check, so a stale non-NULL
+	 * pointer walks past it and faults on sensor->vctx->next_device.
+	 */
+	device->vctx = NULL;
 	minfo("[SEN:D] %s():%d\n", device, __func__, ret);
 	return ret;
 }
